    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is otherwise well-structured and idiomatic: `bulkSignupWarning` mirrors the existing `namesakeCount` pattern exactly (persisted column computed once at creation, getter/setter on `Owner`, schema updates across all four DB dialects, OpenAPI `readOnly` property). That's consistent with how this codebase already captures create-time derived facts, so persisting it rather than computing it in the response layer is defensible here.\n\nOne concrete finding:\n\n- **Duplicated adjacent `if (owner.isNew())` guard** (`ClinicServiceImpl.java:280-286`). The new block opens a second bare `if (owner.isNew())` immediately after the identical guard that sets `namesakeCount`:\n\n  ```java\n  if (owner.isNew()) {\n      owner.setNamesakeCount(countNamesakes(owner));\n  }\n  if (owner.isNew()) {\n      owner.setBulkSignupWarning(\n          countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate) > BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD);\n  }\n  ```\n\n  These two have the exact same, unconditional `isNew()` guard and should be a single block:\n\n  ```java\n  if (owner.isNew()) {\n      owner.setNamesakeCount(countNamesakes(owner));\n      owner.setBulkSignupWarning(\n          countOwnersRegisteredOn(registrationDate) > BULK_SIGNUP_WARNING_THRESHOLD);\n  }\n  ```\n\n  (The earlier `isNew()` blocks at lines 268 and 277 carry extra conditions, so they can't be folded in \u2014 but these two adjacent bare guards are a trivial, avoidable duplication.)",
